            "text": "{\"claims\":[{\"type\":\"scope\",\"location\":\"∀ p : Polynomial ℝ, ∃ C : ℝ\",\"explanation\":\"The formal proposition chooses C after p, so C may depend on the polynomial. The informal wording appears to ask for one constant that works for every degree-1999 polynomial.\",\"prediction\":\"The proposition should be provable by choosing C separately for each eligible p, for example from the ratio of ‖p.eval 0‖ to its positive integral. Such a proof would not establish a uniform constant.\",\"probe_kind\":\"statement\",\"comparison\":null,\"witnesses\":null,\"edit_from\":null,\"edit_to\":null,\"bridge\":\"parse\",\"obligation\":\"Confirm that the informal problem intends a uniform constant; provability of this proposition alone does not distinguish it from the uniform claim, which may also be true.\"}],\"summary\":\"The quantifier order permits a polynomial-dependent constant where the informal statement appears to require a uniform one.\"}"
